WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.629
Film Courage: Let’s take the hypothetical
author who has just written their first novel

00:00:03.629 --> 00:00:06.529
and now they’re looking for an agent.

00:00:06.529 --> 00:00:11.340
What steps should they take, how committed
to that book should they be?

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.650
How free should they be to say “Maybe this
isn’t the one?”

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:18.280
Jennifer Brody: I mean I think it’s always
worth…at the time you write your book, you’ll

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:22.660
be so attached to it and so in love with it,
it will be heartbreaking if you don’t get

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:26.650
agented or if it doesn't sell even though
that’s really typical.

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:31.730
My advice is once you start the querying process,
is to start writing your next book so you

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:35.370
have something else that you’re focused
on and you know that it’s not the end of

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:36.370
the road.

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:40.240
Sometimes it might take an author several
books before they find an agent.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:41.240
That’s not uncommon.

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:48.790
The things I really recommend are workshopping
a lot, taking classes (I teach at the Writing

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:50.740
Pad) which have a workshop component.

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:56.090
Or I workshopped the book that I sold (The
13th Continuum) with Meg Wolitzer who is one

00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:57.380
of my favorite authors.

00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:01.340
I also workshopped with Victor LaValle who
is another one of my favorite authors.

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:07.100
And I would go to conferences and try to meet
people and it really improved my writing.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:11.490
For the querying process it really is finding
the agents who you think are the best fit

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:17.780
that are open to submissions and then really
following their guidelines.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:24.479
Writing a great query letter, spending a lot
time getting that perfect and then publishing

00:01:24.479 --> 00:01:25.640
moves very slowly.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:30.850
So it can be months and months and months
process of even just hearing back from one

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:31.850
person.

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:35.130
And then once you’re going on submission,
it can be months and months and months and

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:38.249
months because they get inundated with so
much material.

00:01:38.249 --> 00:01:42.409
So I think that the best way to handle it
is to then also be working on something for

00:01:42.409 --> 00:01:48.280
yourself otherwise, if an agent comes back
to you and says “Hey, I really like your

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:49.280
book.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:52.509
But I’m not sure I can sell this but I’d
love to see what you do next!”

00:01:52.509 --> 00:01:57.009
So then you can say “Hey, I’m going to
be doing this next” and then you can come

00:01:57.009 --> 00:01:58.009
back to them.

00:01:58.009 --> 00:02:02.429
Film Courage: So with the query letter, did
you have a template that you found where you

00:02:02.429 --> 00:02:04.479
just kind of changed the names and dates?

00:02:04.479 --> 00:02:07.280
Jennifer Brody: No…[laughs] not to that
extent.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:10.009
There are a few good websites online that
will show the format.

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:14.760
So the format is something you really want
to follow and stick to, although I don’t

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:18.819
think you can really do just like changing
things because a lot of it is like writing

00:02:18.819 --> 00:02:26.170
that really ‘hooky’ elevator pitch, making
sure you have a great bio that is concise

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:31.790
and descriptive because when they’re looking
at a query letter, that is the first example

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:33.220
of can you write well?

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:36.370
So the query letter really needs to be written
well.

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:41.340
And I also think ideally in a tone that really
reflects your book, which is to say especially

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:43.030
the synopsis.

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:47.890
If your book is really kind of snappy and
witty, then your synopsis probably should

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:51.799
be somewhat in the same vein to convey that.

00:02:51.799 --> 00:02:56.530
And then in terms of personalizing it, I think
it is really worth while when querying agents

00:02:56.530 --> 00:03:03.410
to personalize to them which is to show some
knowledge about what books they represented,

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:05.730
what types of genres they like (if you were
a big fan).

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:09.360
Like my agent had sold the Devil Wears Prada,
which I absolutely loved.

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:13.840
You know, let them know that you loved this
book and that you’re coming to them because

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:15.750
you know who they are.

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:18.860
You know who their agency is…I think if
you personalize.

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:22.209
And even better if you have a personal connection
or some way.

00:03:22.209 --> 00:03:25.790
Like you’ve been at a conference where they
spoke even, it doesn’t even have to be that

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:30.850
you knew them…just “I saw you at the X
Panel at the AWP Conference and I was really

00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:34.180
impressed and I am coming to you.”

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:36.720
So I think that those things really help a
lot.

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:41.620
It’s just like an editor friend of mine
who used to work at Harper Collins.

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:43.879
She told me in the beginning, “It’s like
dating.”

00:03:43.879 --> 00:03:46.170
She’s like “You’re going to go on a
lot of dates.”

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:51.379
And I think that is really accurate because
you really are looking for the right fit.

00:03:51.379 --> 00:03:56.030
Being unagented is actually better than being
with a bad agent.

00:03:56.030 --> 00:03:58.590
And it doesn’t seem like that would be the
case, but it actually is.

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:02.599
Someone who isn’t a good fit for you, who
isn’t doing your work justice can actually

00:04:02.599 --> 00:04:05.670
do more damage than just not having an agent.

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:11.200
So I just think it’s very important to make
sure that it is the right fit for your material

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.440
and for you because they really need to be
your cheerleader.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:18.320
They really are the person who is going to
be helping you through the crazy process of

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:20.419
trying to find a publisher.

00:04:20.419 --> 00:04:24.600
And ideally also someone who is in it for
the long haul and will stay with you when

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:28.200
your first book doesn’t sell because that’s
so common, who is going to be there for your

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:33.150
next book who will still represent you when
you switch genres and come back and say “Hey,

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:36.370
I’m writing YA Sci-Fi now…can we still
do this?”

00:04:36.370 --> 00:04:40.460
I was very lucky that my agent has stuck by
me and has been amazing through this whole

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:42.630
process.

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:43.630
And she’s responsive.

00:04:43.630 --> 00:04:44.630
She responds to my emails right away.

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:47.020
I know a lot of people will struggle with
that sometimes.

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:51.360
If your agent is not getting back to you,
yeah…I think that’s a real problem.

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:54.310
So I think it’s really important to find
the right person.

00:04:54.310 --> 00:05:00.320
Film Courage: Can you give some other hypotheticals
(such as) how being sort of unagented might

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:01.690
be better than being with the wrong pairing?

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:02.690
Jennifer Brody: Yeah.

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:04.410
There are a bunch of different ways.

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:09.150
I used to go and either meet with or speak
with some pretty well-known authors they used

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:15.490
to say things like “I’m so glad that my
first book didn’t sell.”

00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:17.720
And it’s so hard to see that at the time.

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:22.889
But I was hanging out with Andre Dubus III
who wrote the House of Sand and Fog, which

00:05:22.889 --> 00:05:24.440
was a best selling book.

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:25.840
He’s been on Oprah.

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:28.450
And his dad was the biggest short story author
ever.

00:05:28.450 --> 00:05:30.060
So the guy had connections.

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:34.850
But I believe I asked him “The first book
you ever sold, how many had you written?”

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:35.850
And I think he said 3-4.

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:36.919
It was quite a few.

00:05:36.919 --> 00:05:44.199
And this was a guy who had publishing connections
and a writing degree and all those fancy things.

00:05:44.199 --> 00:05:48.069
And the thing about it when you’re first
writing, you’re developing your craft and

00:05:48.069 --> 00:05:49.069
you get better.

00:05:49.069 --> 00:05:52.810
So a lot of times the first thing you write
isn’t in the sweet spot of what you’re

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:54.300
supposed to be writing.

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:56.490
And a lot of times you will look back and
be like “Wow!

00:05:56.490 --> 00:05:57.690
I really was undeveloped.

00:05:57.690 --> 00:06:01.610
It really was better that this was the stage
where I sold a book.

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:04.759
And being an author is a little different
from some other creative professions.

00:06:04.759 --> 00:06:07.919
Because you don’t really age out of it to
the same degree.

00:06:07.919 --> 00:06:13.540
I mean sure, publishing loves the young 22-year-old
who has the hit book like Veronica Roth who

00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:14.540
wrote Divergent (Series).

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:18.310
Yeah, they love that narrative but a lot of
times authors are going to write their best

00:06:18.310 --> 00:06:19.550
work when they get older.

00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:25.370
30, 40, 50, 60…I mean Frank McCourt who
wrote Angela’s Ashes, I think he wrote that

00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:26.699
when he was about 50 or 60.

00:06:26.699 --> 00:06:29.120
That’s one of my favorite memoirs of all
time.

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:32.889
A lot of my favorite authors who are producing
work are not young people…

00:06:32.889 --> 00:06:34.360
Film Courage: Ursula Le Guin…

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:35.750
Jennifer Brody: Ursula K. Le Guin is amazing.

00:06:35.750 --> 00:06:36.840
I am obsessed with Stephen King.

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:39.922
I think he’s a genius and I still read all
of his books.

00:06:39.922 --> 00:06:42.090
And he’s still publishing a ton.

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:43.340
I still read Anne Rice.

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:44.479
But then there are other people.

00:06:44.479 --> 00:06:47.490
I know Philip Roth retired only recently.

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:50.610
I mean he was writing books up until a couple
years ago.

00:06:50.610 --> 00:06:53.840
And I think a lot times they are producing
their best work.

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:58.340
Like Cormac McCarthy is writing some of his
best books within the last 10 years and he

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:00.020
is not a young man.

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:03.889
So I think that we develop over the course
of our writing careers.

00:07:03.889 --> 00:07:08.759
I like to look at it as, I’d hope to write
many, many, many books and I hope to get better

00:07:08.759 --> 00:07:09.759
as I do.

00:07:09.759 --> 00:07:11.050
So really kind of cultivating that.

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:12.050
Question for the Viewers: What is your experience
in seeking a literary agent?

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:12.055
Any insights you can share?

